# Multi-Hop Example

Chain through intermediate relays to reach targets that cannot connect directly to the entry node. A relay in the middle bridges the gap — the DMZ host (`10.0.1.10`) connects to your machine (`10.0.0.5`) and listens for the next hop. Once the tunnel is established, traffic is routed to the networks the internal host can reach — in this example, `192.168.1.0/24` and `192.168.2.0/24`.

{% steps %}
{% step title="Attacker" number=1 %}
An [entry node](/concepts) listening for the first hop.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="DMZ Host" number=2 %}
A [relay node](/concepts) connecting to the attacker and listening for the next hop.

{% codeblock code="wallhack relay --connect 10.0.0.5:443 --listen :6565" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Internal Host" number=3 %}
An [exit node](/concepts) connecting back to the relay.

{% codeblock code="wallhack exit --connect 10.0.1.10:6565" /%}
{% /step %}

{% step title="Configure Routes" number=4 %}
Add routes for the networks accessible through the internal host.

```bash
ip route add 192.168.1.0/24 dev tun0
ip route add 192.168.2.0/24 dev tun0
```
{% /step %}
{% /steps %}

{% trafficflow flow="10.0.0.5 (you) -> 10.0.1.10 (DMZ) -> internal host -> 192.168.1.0/24, 192.168.2.0/24" label="Traffic Flow:" /%}
